WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.628
MARTIN: Hey, Craig.

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:03.420
CRAIG: Hey, Martin.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.460
What are you working on?

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.310
MARTIN: I built a web app
and a mobile app that display

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.290
info about famous artists.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.680
It is backed by a REST
API running on Cloud Run.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.660
But users are reporting
that it's sometimes slow.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.750
Can you help me speed it up?

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.770
CRAIG: Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.020
It sounds like you might
need a caching layer.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.210
Let me show you how it's done.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:24.840
MARTIN: Great.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.440
MARTIN: All right,
Craig, so here is my API.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.400
If I hit the URL here, it
takes three seconds to respond.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.230
CRAIG: Three seconds?

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.038
What does your code look like?

00:00:46.038 --> 00:00:47.760
MARTIN: [LAUGHS]
Yeah, here it is.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.910
This call here runs a
complex database query.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.400
It takes several
seconds to complete.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.250
CRAIG: I see.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.390
So you could try to save
recently accessed artists

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.240
in a cache, and that would
make your slow database query

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.030
run less often.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.932
And it looks like your
data structure here

00:01:04.932 --> 00:01:05.640
is pretty simple.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.740
It's just an ID that
points to an object.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.027
And that really lends
itself to a key value cache.

00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:12.860
MARTIN: Sounds good.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.510
Can you show me how to do it?

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:15.620
CRAIG: Absolutely.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.800
Click Memorystore in
the Cloud console.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.970
Memorystore is built to handle
exactly this kind of caching.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.980
MARTIN: I will
pick Redis for now.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.910
And I'm enabling
Memorystore in this project.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:30.510
CRAIG: Great.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:35.820
Now, click Create Instance,
and give it an instance name

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.390
and display name.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.380
And then for the rest of the
fields, the defaults are fine,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.000
so you should be able to
click the Create button.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.250
Now your Redis server
is being initialized.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.740
And this server sits in
a virtual private cloud.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.315
So it's not accessible
from the outside internet.

00:01:52.315 --> 00:01:53.940
That means the next
thing we need to do

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.100
is set up a connector
so that your Cloud Run

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.430
service can connect to
this virtual private cloud.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.640
And to do that, first
pick VPC network

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.740
in the navigation sidebar.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.000
Then click Serverless
VPC Access.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.970
Now, click Create Connector.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.960
Give it the name
my-vpc-connector.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.830
Under Network, pick default.
And under subnet, Pick Custom IP

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:22.860
range.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.260
Now in the text box, enter the
suggested value of 10.8.0.0.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.490
That works great.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.860
And click the Create button.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.380
Now, let's head back
over to Memorystore,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.745
and copy the IP address
of the Redis instance

00:02:36.745 --> 00:02:37.620
that we just created.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.890
Now, in your deployment script,
add $REDIS_HOST, $REDIS_PORT,

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.526
and vpc-connector as
environment variables.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.030
MARTIN: Now that is done.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.300
I will enter the code
snippet you sent me.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.280
CRAIG: Awesome.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.700
That subnet creates
a Redis client

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.500
object so that
your other code can

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.390
use that to access the cache.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.030
And then instead of calling
getArtistsFromDatabase,

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.330
we can call a new function,
getArtistFromCache.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.170
Now, in this new
getArtistFromCache method,

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.670
the first three
lines simply make

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.100
the Redis client use promises
instead of callbacks.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.280
And this just makes your
code easier to read.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.400
Then the code checks to see if
the artist with the given ID

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.730
is in the cache.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.810
If it isn't, it calls
getArtistsFromDatabase and puts

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.970
that artist in the cache.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.520
Finally, it reads the
artist's record from the cache

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.270
and returns it.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.470
MARTIN: I'm deploying the
updated code to Cloud Run.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:40.430
Very good.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.050
The code is now deployed.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.510
I'm hitting the URL now.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.803
Oh, it's still as
slow as before, Craig.

00:03:47.803 --> 00:03:49.970
CRAIG: Yes, of course,
because the artist is not yet

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.020
in the cache on
that first request.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:57.690
But if you reload the page, I
think it'll be a lot faster.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.510
MARTIN: I'm reloading.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.430
Oh, that was a lot faster.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:02.990
Cool.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.260
But what if I need
to update that artist

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:07.060
record in the database?

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.670
CRAIG: Well, then you'll need
to update it in the cache, too.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.500
I sent you some
more code for that.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.570
Did you get it?

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.060
MARTIN: Ah, yes.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.560
Let's see.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.607
Entering it now.

00:04:18.607 --> 00:04:19.149
CRAIG: Great.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.880
So this method uses
the request payload

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.330
to update both the cache value
and any database records.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.820
So the cache is never stale.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.180
MARTIN: Excellent.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.980
Let's redeploy the API.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.420
Now back to Postman.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.900
Here, I have a put request that
will add the field movement

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.150
to Frida Kahlo's artist record.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.378
She belonged to the
surrealism movement.

00:04:43.378 --> 00:04:45.170
CRAIG: Well, that's
perfect, because you're

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:50.582
going to have a surreally fast
API with all this caching.

00:04:50.582 --> 00:04:54.410
MARTIN: [LAUGHS] Maybe don't
quit your day job, Craig.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.260
Now, to reread
that artist's data.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.050
Ah, and here it is with a
new movement field added.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.080
And it was very quick
served from the cache.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.870
Thanks for helping me
speed up my API, Craig.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:06.853
CRAIG: Happy to help.

00:05:06.853 --> 00:05:08.270
Now, right now,
your code is using

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.430
the read-through and
write-through caching

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:11.240
strategies.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.490
And they work pretty well
for most applications.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.870
But you might also
want to search

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.090
online for some other
caching patterns in case

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:19.210
those work better for your app.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.740
Now, also remember that
your code can never

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.810
count on this object
being in the cache.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.270
That's why we still have
the database backing it up.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:30.140
When a cache fills up, it can
kick out objects at any time.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:30.950
MARTIN: Oh, right.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.230
Thank you for that
reminder, Craig.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.090
And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:36.710
You will find a
link to the code and

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:39.710
to the Memorystore documentation
in the description below.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.300
Please let us know
in the comments

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.940
if you found this episode useful
or if you have suggestions

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.705
for future episodes.

00:05:45.705 --> 00:05:46.580
CRAIG: Happy caching.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.930
